ntdll: a memory log for a running program

Arknights: Endfield's memory footprint grew by about 3 GB a minute, to 50 GB on a 24 GB Mac,
in 128 MB blocks that nothing identified from outside (a debugger cannot be attached to a game
with anti-cheat, and Wine's own +virtual trace is far too much output for a game).

With FINEWINE_MEMORY_TRACE set to a folder, each process now writes "<program>-<pid>.log"
there: one line for every reservation, commit, decommit, release, reset and section mapping of
FINEWINE_MEMORY_TRACE_MIN megabytes or more (default 8), with the thread and the modules that
the words on the caller's stack point into, nearest first; and every five seconds the count and
size of all operations so far, the process's footprint, and how much was decommitted in pieces
that share a 16K host page with memory still in use and so could not be given back. A process
that never does anything that large leaves no file. Without the variable nothing runs.

FineWine's "Play with Memory Log" starts a program this way. tests/runtime/memtrace.c checks it.

